# Shared project workflow

Follow the user's current request and the project's own instructions. Keep progress reports short and preserve existing work.

## Start a session

- Find the existing project with `desk projects` or `map <words>`.
- Read `desk context <project>`, then inspect the actual project files.
- Check `desk list` and coordinate overlapping edits directly.
- Register the real work folder with `desk start <project-or-folder> "<task>" <agent>`.
- Use a native Git worktree when isolation is useful, and register its actual path.

## Leave the next session a handoff

- Save progress with `desk note <task> "<result>" --next "<next action>" --checks "<what was verified>"`.
- Finish with `desk done <task> "<result>" --next "<remaining work or None>" --checks "<what was verified>"`.
- Pause unfinished work with `desk pause <task> "<reason>" --next "<next action>"`.
- Resume a paused task explicitly with `desk resume <task>`.
- Record checks that were actually run. Explain when verification was not run.

## Find and preserve context

- `mem <words>` searches current shared notes and handoffs; `mem --history <words>` includes older references.
- Current project files establish what is implemented. The task board records reported work. Notes hold reusable explanations and decisions.
- Use the existing project and document folders. Keep credentials and private application histories out of shared notes.
- Native applications own their settings and model choices. Git commits, merges, publishing and cleanup remain explicit actions within the user's request.
